#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:55
msgid "You know from session 1 and 3: a string is a **sequence** of characters; `len(s)`, `s[0]`, `s[-1]`, `s[2:5]` work like on a list; `s[2:5]` is `SUBSTR(s, 3, 3)`. And a string **cannot change** (it is immutable): every method returns a **new** string."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:69
msgid "This is the most common mistake of today: a method call without `=`. `repr()` (or `!r` in an f-string) shows the quotes, so you can see the spaces."
