#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1055
msgid "This part is not covered in class. It is for reading at home: what an encoding is, why Cyrillic text sometimes comes out as `РЎРѕС„РёСЏ`, and how to write Cyrillic names in Latin letters."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1059
msgid "Encodings: UTF-8, cp1251 and Cyrillic"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1061
msgid "A file on disk is **bytes**, not text. An **encoding** is the rule that turns text into bytes and back. The same text gives different bytes in different encodings:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1074
msgid "**UTF-8** - every character of every language; the standard of the web, of Python source files and of most new files."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1076
msgid "**cp1251** (Windows-1251) - the old Windows encoding for Cyrillic. Exports from old systems and Excel files saved as \"CSV\" on a Bulgarian Windows are often cp1251."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1079
msgid "Reading with the wrong encoding gives either an error, or **mojibake** - wrong letters:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1090
msgid "That is why every `open()` in this course says `encoding=\"utf-8\"`: without it, Python 3.12 / 3.13 on Windows uses the Windows encoding of the computer (often cp1252 or cp1251), and the same script gives different results on different computers. (Python 3.15 uses UTF-8 by default everywhere.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1095
msgid "The file `regions_bg.csv` is saved in **cp1251**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1105
msgid "`errors=\"replace\"` puts `?` (or `�`) instead of a byte that cannot be read, instead of stopping. Use it only to **look** at a file, never to load data."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1108
msgid "One more detail: Excel saves \"CSV UTF-8\" with a **BOM** - three bytes `EF BB BF` at the start. Read such a file with `encoding=\"utf-8-sig\"`, or the first column name gets an invisible character in front:"
msgstr ""

