#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1144
msgid "Cyrillic names in Latin letters"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1146
msgid "Bulgaria has an official transliteration (the Transliteration Act of 2009): `ж` -> `zh`, `ц` -> `ts`, `ч` -> `ch`, `ш` -> `sh`, `щ` -> `sht`, `ъ` -> `a`, `ю` -> `yu`, `я` -> `ya`, ... A dictionary of single letters to strings, used with `translate`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1163
msgid "`str.maketrans` accepts a dictionary of **one character -> any string**, so one letter can become two or three. (The full rules have a few exceptions, for example `-ия` at the end of a word is `-ia`: `София` -> `Sofia`, not `Sofiya`.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-processing.md:1167
msgid "Another trap of text from many sources: the same letter can be stored in two ways - `\"й\"` as one character, or `\"и\"` plus a combining mark. They look the same, but are not `==`. `unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", s)` brings both to the same form:"
msgstr ""

